    "evidence_text": "They resemble legitimate users who are difficult to identify.\n\nUnlike previous\n\nThere is a considerable amount of previous work on spammer detection on social platforms.\n\nResearcher from Twitter Inc.\n\ncollect bot accounts and perform analysis on the user behavior and user profile features.\n\nBut this may not be applicable to the current scenario of smart spammers.\n\nin Section 6.\n\nObservation\tAfter exploring the homepages of a substantial number of spammers, we have two observations.\n\n1) social spammers can be divided into two categories.\n\nContent polluters concentrate on certain topics.\n\n1 shows the topic distribution\n\nThis is because there are more content polluters than fake accounts.\n\nIn other word, spammers in this dataset tend to concentrate on limited topics.\n\nTherefore we can detect spammers by means of the difference of their topic distribution patterns.\n\nThis is our first test dataset.\n\nThe spammers are all purchased commercially from multiple vendors on the Internet.\n\nWe checked them manually and collected 802 suitable \"smart\" spammers accounts.\n\nAfter preprocessing steps, the\n\nPrecision is the ratio of selected accounts that are spammers.\n\nRecall is the ratio of spammers that are detected so.\n\nIt is caused by the incorrectly classified spammers who are mostly news source after our manual check.",
